2026-07-30 (continued) -- first public release
------------------------------------------------
Pushed to GitHub for the first time (public: github.com/zflow-byte/ttyt).
Before pushing: independent security review (security subagent), full
`git log -p --all` sweep for secrets across every commit (clean), added a
missing `LICENSE` (MIT, matching Cargo.toml's declared license), and
expanded `.gitignore` (editor dirs, swap files, defensive config/history
entries). Review found and fixed two real gaps: default redaction
patterns missed SNMPv3 auth/priv passphrases and VRRP/HSRP plaintext
auth (added `\bauth\b`/`\bpriv\b`/`\bauthentication\b`/`\bcleartext\b`),
and default dangerous-command patterns were Cisco-IOS-shaped only --
Comware's `reboot` and JunOS's `request system reboot`/`zeroize` sailed
past the confirm-before-send guard unconfirmed (added all three).
Regression tests added for both directly against `Redactor`/
`DangerousCommandGuard`. README's status line lost its reference to
`outputs/2026-07-29-smart-console-plan.md` "in the parent workspace" --
that file lives in a private multi-project workspace this repo was
extracted from, not something that ships with the public repo.

Tagged and pushed `v0.1.0`. Filled in `Formula/ttyt.rb`'s real GitHub
path and, once the tag existed, its real `sha256` (was a placeholder) --
verified by actually building and testing from the real release tarball,
not just hashing it. Published a proper tap
(github.com/zflow-byte/homebrew-ttyt) so `brew tap zflow-byte/ttyt &&
brew install ttyt` works as a real single-command install; along the way
discovered current Homebrew (6.x) rejects installing a bare formula file
outside a tap, and requires one-time `brew trust` for a third-party tap
before its formulae will load -- both documented in README. Also found
and fixed a PATH-shadow conflict on the test machine: `~/.cargo/bin`
(from earlier `cargo install --path crates/ttyt-cli` testing this
session) sat ahead of Homebrew's bin directory, silently shadowing the
Homebrew-installed binary -- removed the cargo-installed copy so `ttyt`
resolves to the Homebrew one, and documented the gotcha in README for
anyone in the same situation.

**Real bug found via actual hardware testing (v0.1.0), fixed in v0.1.1:**
`widgets/console.rs` built its scrollback+input-line content as a
`Paragraph`, which top-aligns by default. With little/no scrollback (a
freshly connected or quiet device -- exactly the common case right after
`connect`), the rendered lines were far shorter than the pane's height,
so the input line rendered a few rows below the pane's top border while
the reported terminal cursor position stayed calculated as the pane's
actual last row regardless -- a visible gap between the "> " prompt text
and the blinking cursor, with no way to tell what mode the session was in
or how much had been typed before Enter would submit it. Existing test
coverage never caught this because `widgets::mod::tests::render_to_string`
flattens the entire screen buffer into one string before asserting,
checking only that content appears *somewhere*, never *which row*.

Fixed by padding `lines` with leading blank lines up to the pane's full
height before building the `Paragraph`, so the input line always lands
on the actual last row, matching how a real terminal fills upward from
the bottom rather than top-aligning. Added a real regression test
(`input_line_renders_on_the_pane_last_row_even_with_no_scrollback`) that
checks row *position* directly via `TestBackend`'s buffer content at a
specific row -- confirmed it actually catches the bug by reverting the
fix locally and re-running (test failed as expected: cursor row was
blank), then restoring it (test passed).

Bumped workspace version 0.1.0 -> 0.1.1 (Cargo.toml + Cargo.lock), tagged
and pushed `v0.1.1`, re-verified full fmt/clippy -D warnings/test (3x)
clean: 222 tests (152 core + 67 ui + 3 cli). Formula/ttyt.rb's `url`/
`sha256` updated to point at the real v0.1.1 tarball -- v0.1.0 remains
tagged in git history as the record of what was actually reviewed and
published first, not deleted or force-moved.

**Real hardware gap found and fixed in v0.1.2: `--More--`/pagination
support.** Testing against real device output surfaced a case worse than
"the UI doesn't handle it": `LineAssembler::feed` only ever emitted
content terminated by `\n`, and a `--More--`-style prompt never sends
one -- the device blocks waiting for a single keystroke, so that content
sat invisibly in the assembler's internal buffer, only ever surfacing via
`flush()` on disconnect. Separately, `Session::on_key`'s Enter handler was
a no-op when `input` was empty, so a bare Enter (which a user might try,
thinking it pages through) sent nothing at all -- the session looked
hung with no way to tell what was going on.

Fixed with full interactive support (the more invasive of two options
considered; the alternative -- auto-disabling pagination on connect via
vendor `terminal length 0`-style commands -- was rejected in favor of
handling the real prompt):

- `LineAssembler::feed` now returns `Vec<AssembledOutput>`
  (`Line`/`PaginationPrompt`) instead of `Vec<String>`. After draining
  complete lines, the remaining unterminated buffer is checked against a
  small set of known markers (`--More--` for Cisco/Dell OS10/Aruba CX,
  `---- More ----` for Comware, `---(more` as a prefix match for JunOS's
  `---(more N%)---` variants) and drained on match rather than left to
  mangle into the next line once a newline eventually arrives. Detection
  is deliberately generic/vendor-agnostic rather than routed through
  `VendorPlugin`: a long banner can itself page before vendor detection
  finishes, and if detection had to run first that would be a permanent
  deadlock (the device is stuck waiting for a keystroke detection could
  never unblock).
- New `SessionEvent::PaginationPrompt(String)`; `reader_loop` publishes it
  instead of `RawLine` when the assembler recognizes a marker. Recorded to
  the session transcript the same as any other output line (subject to
  the same redaction); the detector explicitly ignores it (nothing to
  classify).
- New `ConnectionHandle::write_raw(byte: u8)`, sharing `write_line`'s
  `write_pending` wrapping so this path doesn't reintroduce the
  reader/writer starvation that wrapping was originally added to fix --
  sends exactly one byte, no trailing newline.
- New `ttyt-ui` `Mode::Pagination`: entered when a `PaginationPrompt`
  event arrives (pushed to scrollback first, same as any other output),
  and every keystroke while active converts straight to a raw byte
  instead of going through `input`/`pending_submit` -- `Enter` sends
  `\r` (matching a real terminal, not `write_line`'s `\n`, which doesn't
  advance paging on every vendor), `Esc` cancels back to `Normal` with
  nothing sent. `Ctrl+C`/`Ctrl+N`/`Ctrl+R`/`Ctrl+P` all stay reachable
  during pagination mode exactly as they already are for the other three
  modes -- deliberately, since the marker match is a plain substring
  check and a false positive (e.g. `--More--` appearing as literal text
  in a banner) would otherwise leave the next keystroke injected as a
  raw byte into a production device with no way out.
- New dedicated `raw_key_tx` channel through `ttyt_ui::run` alongside
  `submit_tx`/`disconnect_tx` (not folded into `submit_tx`'s `String`
  payload, so neither call site has to distinguish a submitted line from
  a raw byte inside a shared type); `commands.rs`'s `connect()` forwards
  it to `ConnectionHandle::write_raw`, `replay()` drains and discards it
  (no live device to write to, same as the other two channels there).

Verified end-to-end over a real PTY pair (no interactive keyboard/TTY
available in this environment to drive the actual binary the way a human
would over `socat`, so this is the same real-OS-file-descriptor proof the
existing `real_pty_bytes_flow_through_to_raw_line_events` test already
uses, extended through the write path):
`real_pty_more_prompt_and_raw_key_response_round_trip` writes an
unterminated `--More--` into one side of the pair, confirms the
`PaginationPrompt` event, calls `write_raw(b' ')`, and confirms the other
side receives exactly that one byte -- nothing else, no newline.

Full fmt/clippy -D warnings/test clean: 237 tests (160 core + 74 ui + 3
cli), up from 222 in v0.1.1. README documents the new pagination
behavior (keybindings + a "Known limitations" note on the substring-match
false-positive tradeoff). Bumped workspace version 0.1.1 -> 0.1.2
(Cargo.toml + Cargo.lock), tagged and pushed `v0.1.2`; Formula/ttyt.rb's
`url`/`sha256` updated to point at the real v0.1.2 tarball in both this
repo and the published tap (github.com/zflow-byte/homebrew-ttyt).

**Realtime output streaming, v0.1.3.** User request: connect/configure
sessions should show realtime raw output from the device, not just after
each line completes. Until now `LineAssembler::feed` only ever emitted
content once a `\n` arrived (or a pagination marker matched) -- a line
still being received (mid-way through a slow `show tech-support`, or any
prompt that never sends a trailing newline) sat invisibly in the internal
buffer the whole time, the same category of gap the v0.1.2 `--More--`
work fixed for pagination prompts specifically, now generalized.

- `LineAssembler::feed` gained a third `AssembledOutput::Partial(String)`
  variant: after draining complete lines and checking for a pagination
  marker, if anything is still buffered with no newline yet, it's emitted
  as a live preview of the buffer's current content so far. The next
  `feed()` call either replaces it with a longer `Partial` (more bytes,
  still no newline), finalizes it into a `Line` (newline arrived), or
  turns it into a `PaginationPrompt` (marker matched) -- never both a
  stale `Partial` and its resolution in the same output.
- New `SessionEvent::PartialLine(String)`, published by `reader_loop`
  alongside `RawLine`/`PaginationPrompt`. Two places explicitly ignore it,
  both documented inline with the reasoning: `recorder::run` (a
  redaction pattern like `\bpassword\b` matches whole keywords, so a
  fragment like "username admin passw" would pass through unredacted in
  the log even though the eventual complete line matches and gets
  `[REDACTED]` -- only the finished `RawLine` is ever recorded) and
  `detector::run` (classifying a half-arrived line risks a premature
  `parse_prompt`/`parse_output` match that then has to be corrected once
  the rest arrives -- flicker for no benefit).
- New `ttyt-ui` `Session::partial_output: Option<String>`, set by
  `PartialLine` and cleared by `RawLine` (superseded by the authoritative
  line), `PaginationPrompt` (buffer was already drained), and
  `ConnectionStateChanged` transitioning to `Disconnected`/`Reconnecting`
  (nothing more is coming to complete it -- left in place it would look
  like a live line on a dead connection).
- `widgets/console.rs` renders the partial line between scrollback and
  the input line. This surfaced a second, unrelated row-budget bug caught
  before it shipped (advisor review flagged it): the pane only ever
  reserved one row for the input line; with scrollback already filling
  the pane, adding a partial line without reserving a second row handed
  `Paragraph` one more line than the pane had rows, clipping the input
  line off the bottom while the cursor (still calculated as the pane's
  last row) pointed at nothing drawn there -- same failure shape as the
  v0.1.1 top-alignment bug, different cause. Fixed by reserving two rows
  instead of one whenever a partial line is present. Verified as a real
  regression test (not just plausible-looking): reverted the two-row
  reservation, confirmed the new test failed with exactly the predicted
  symptom, restored it, confirmed it passed.

Two existing real-PTY tests
(`real_pty_bytes_flow_through_to_raw_line_events`,
`real_pty_more_prompt_and_raw_key_response_round_trip`) were made robust
against a real PTY splitting a write mid-marker/mid-line, which would
now interleave a `PartialLine` where the tests asserted a bare event
sequence -- a `recv_skipping_partial` test helper drains and skips
`PartialLine`s before asserting the event under test (this was flagged
by review before it caused an intermittent CI failure, not caught by
one). A new dedicated real-PTY test,
`real_pty_partial_line_streams_before_the_newline_arrives`, directly
proves the live-streaming claim itself: two separate writes with no
newline in between produce a `PartialLine` for the first write before
the `RawLine` for the completed line arrives.

Known tradeoff, noted rather than mitigated: this roughly doubles event-
bus traffic in the worst case (up to one more event per `read()` call).
`recorder::run` treats a lagged receiver as "log a warning, skip it,
keep going" (`RecvError::Lagged`), so higher traffic in the same
1024-slot bus makes a dropped log line marginally more likely under a
very chatty device. Likely fine at real serial baud rates; flagged here
so it's a known tradeoff if a transcript ever comes up short, not a
surprise.

Full fmt/clippy -D warnings/test clean: 240 tests (162 core + 75 ui + 3
cli), up from 237 in v0.1.2. README gained a "Realtime output" section
and the pagination false-negative bullet was softened to match the new
reality (an unlisted marker variant's prompt text is now visible live,
just without passthrough -- not invisible like before). Bumped workspace
version 0.1.2 -> 0.1.3, tagged and pushed `v0.1.3`; Formula/ttyt.rb's
`url`/`sha256` updated to point at the real v0.1.3 tarball in both this
repo and the published tap.

**Two more real bugs found via advisor review immediately after v0.1.3
shipped, fixed in v0.1.4.** All of v0.1.3's own tests used short strings
("Router#show ru", "Switch> "), which never exercised the case network
engineers actually hit constantly: a long unterminated line with no
newline until it finishes.

- **Truncation direction.** `Paragraph` without `.wrap()` renders a line
  from its start and clips at the pane's width. Cisco `copy`
  running-config/firmware-upload progress (thousands of `!` marks on one
  line, no newline until done) would render its first ~38 characters
  once and then appear frozen while the device kept working --
  indistinguishable from an actual hang, which is exactly the symptom
  this whole feature exists to rule out. Fixed by showing the *tail* of
  an over-wide partial line instead of the head -- what's new is always
  at the end, which is the only place progress is visible on a fixed-
  width pane. Scrollback lines are untouched (head-truncation there is
  normal, matches how a real terminal's history scrolls).
- **Embedded `\r`.** A `\r`-based progress counter (some vendors'
  percentage displays) overwrites the same terminal column rather than
  appending a new one; `Line` doesn't interpret raw `\r` bytes as cursor
  movement the way a real terminal emulator would, so `"10%\r20%\r30%"`
  would render as the stages mashed together ("10%20%30%") instead of
  the current value. Fixed by collapsing the partial preview to only the
  segment after the last `\r` before applying the tail-truncation above.

Both fixes live entirely in `widgets/console.rs`'s new
`partial_line_preview` helper -- deliberately not in `LineAssembler`,
which keeps its existing line-assembly contract (what gets logged,
redacted, and eventually shown in scrollback once a real newline
arrives) completely unchanged. Only the still-growing on-screen preview
is affected.

Full fmt/clippy -D warnings/test clean: 245 tests (162 core + 80 ui + 3
cli), up from 240 in v0.1.3, including a `TestBackend` regression test
proving a 100+-character partial line's tail (not its stale head)
actually renders on screen. Bumped workspace version 0.1.3 -> 0.1.4,
tagged and pushed `v0.1.4`; Formula/ttyt.rb's `url`/`sha256` updated to
point at the real v0.1.4 tarball in both this repo and the published tap.

**Ctrl+L didn't clear a stuck partial preview, v0.1.5.** User reported
real hardware testing against a FortiGate: the word "Current" appearing
repeated down the sidebar area, and Ctrl+L not making it go away. The
recorded session log did not contain "Current" anywhere, confirming
whatever was on screen was not real device content flowing through
`SessionEvent` (the recorder sees every `RawLine`/`PaginationPrompt`,
never anything rendering-only) -- so the repeated-per-row visual itself
was very likely a terminal-scrollback/copy artifact from how the
screen text was captured (ttyt redraws in place via cursor movement,
not new lines, so scrolling back through a terminal's own history and
selecting text can interleave content from several different redraw
frames), not something `ttyt` itself drew that way. That part was not
reproduced and remains unconfirmed.

What *was* confirmed by reading the code directly: `Session::
clear_console` (bound to `Ctrl+L`) only ever cleared `scrollback` --
`partial_output` (the live realtime-preview field added in v0.1.3) was
never touched by it. A `--More--`-heavy session streaming lots of
partial content could plausibly leave a stale preview on screen that
`Ctrl+L` would not remove, exactly matching "clear ยังค้างอยู่." Fixed by
clearing `partial_output` alongside `scrollback` in `clear_console`.
Verified as a genuine fix the same way as prior UI regressions this
project has shipped: reverted the one-line change, confirmed the new
test failed with the exact predicted symptom, restored it, confirmed it
passed.

Full fmt/clippy -D warnings/test clean: 246 tests (162 core + 81 ui + 3
cli), up from 245 in v0.1.4. Bumped workspace version 0.1.4 -> 0.1.5,
tagged and pushed `v0.1.5`; Formula/ttyt.rb's `url`/`sha256` updated to
point at the real v0.1.5 tarball in both this repo and the published tap.

**Root cause of the "Current" corruption found and fixed, v0.1.6.** The
v0.1.5 fix addressed a real but secondary bug (`Ctrl+L` not clearing a
stuck partial preview); the user reported the corruption itself
unchanged after upgrading. A second, clearer hardware capture (full
`ttyt` window with box-drawing borders intact, not just a text
fragment) made the actual mechanism identifiable: the Sessions pane's
right border was missing on exactly the affected rows, and console text
was overtyped mid-word with the *same length* ("Maxonumber" for "Max
number", "vir ual" for "virtual") -- not corrupted or dropped, *typed
over*.

That's the signature of a literal `\r` (carriage return) reaching the
real terminal. FortiGate's `--More--` paging erases its own prompt with
a `\r`-erase-`\r` sequence before printing the next line (confirmed via
a real hardware byte capture: `"\r        \rvirtual domain: root\r\n"`).
`LineAssembler` only ever strips a *trailing* `\r` immediately before
`\n` -- embedded ones survive into the assembled line verbatim, by
design (this is correct: the log/redaction contract should reflect what
the device actually sent). `widgets/console.rs` handed that string
straight to `ratatui::text::Line` with no sanitizing. `Line` treats
every character as literal, non-advancing cell content -- it is not a
terminal emulator -- but crossterm then writes those cells to the real
terminal verbatim, and the *real terminal* interprets `\r` as "return to
column 0." Everything ttyt drew after that point in the same paint
landed at the wrong columns, overwriting the Sessions pane's border and
whatever text was already there -- exactly matching the report, down to
the fact that Ctrl+L had no effect (it isn't stale state; the corrupted
line is re-rendered from `scrollback` on every single frame) and why it
started exactly at the first `--More--` (the first point FortiGate
sends this erase sequence).

An earlier attempt to diagnose this asked whether the string "Current"
appeared in the session's recorded log file, reasoning that the
recorder only ever sees real `RawLine`/`PaginationPrompt` content, never
rendering-only state -- if absent, that would point away from real
device data. The user reported it was absent. That check was
misleading: `"Current Security Level: High"` is itself a real,
literal line from the device's own `get system status` output, so it
must be in the log regardless of this bug -- the earlier answer most
likely came from checking the wrong log file (a different day directory
or session), not genuine evidence the corruption wasn't real device
content. Recorded here as a caution for next time: a log-presence check
is only as good as confirming it's actually the right log file first.

Fixed with a new `sanitize_for_display` helper in `widgets/console.rs`,
applied to every scrollback line (previously handed to `Line::from`
completely unsanitized) and generalized from the `\r`-collapse logic
`partial_line_preview` already had for the live preview (v0.1.4):
collapses to the segment after the last `\r` (what a real terminal would
show once every erase-and-redraw in a sequence has played out), then
strips any other control character (backspace, stray ANSI bytes, ...)
that survives, since none of those have rendering meaning to `Line`
either and risk the same class of corruption. Deliberately kept at the
display layer, not in `LineAssembler` -- the log/redaction contract is
unchanged; a raw `\r` in a log file is harmless.

Verified with a `LineAssembler` test confirming the embedded `\r`
genuinely survives into the assembled line (documenting that this is
`LineAssembler`'s correct, unchanged behavior, not the bug) and a
`TestBackend` regression test asserting the real hardware sequence
renders with **zero control characters in any cell** across the whole
buffer, not just that the visible text happens to look right --
confirmed as a genuine catch via the usual revert-and-confirm-fail
cycle (reverted the scrollback sanitize call, test failed with `found
"\r"` at the exact predicted cell, restored it, test passed).

